Let $v(x)=\sqrt{x}$, then $\sqrt{u(x)}=v\Bigl(u(x)\Bigr)$, or $(v\circ u)(x)$.

Therefore, the derivative of $\sqrt{u(x)}$ can be found using **the chain rule**:

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\dfrac{d}{dx}\sqrt{u(x)}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[v\Bigl(u(x)\Bigr)\right]&&\gray{\text{Let }v(x)=\sqrt{x}}
\\\\
&=v'\Bigl(u(x)\Bigr)\cdot u'(x)&&\gray{\text{The chain rule}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{1}{2}[u(x)]^{^{-\scriptsize\dfrac{1}{2}}}\cdot u'(x)&&\gray{\dfrac{d}{dx}\sqrt{x}=\dfrac{1}{2}x^{^{-\scriptsize\dfrac{1}{2}}}}
\end{align}$
